 voting. \nThe Bill, as amended, was passed by the required majority. \nMESSAGE FROM LOK SABHA \nRe: Supplementary agenda for Lokpal Bill and the  \nWhistleblower's Protection Bill \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA (Jharkhand): Sir, what about the Supplementary \nBusiness for the Lokpal Bill? \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: It is already circulated. No? \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: No, Sir; the Supplementary Agenda for the Lokpal \nBill has not yet come. \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: But the Supplementary Agenda for the \nWhistleblower's Protection Bill has come. \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: Sir, today, we would like to take up the Lokpal \nBill first. ...(Interruption)... Otherwise, you may take up the Bill \ntomorrow. I am told that at 5 o'clock, we have to adjourn the House. We \ncan take up the Lokpal Bill tomorrow at 11 o'clock. \nTHE MINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS (SHRI PAWAN KUMAR BANSAL): Sir, \nany Bill can be taken up; it is left to the Government. \n...(Interruption)... My appeal to the hon. Members is... \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: No, this extension of the Session for three days \nwas for a particular purpose. Now, the Bill is passed in that House and \nsince morning we are waiting for that Bill. We are told that the \nPresident of India was not here and to get the signature it took time. \nBut, till now, there is no Supplementary Agenda! If you circulate that, \nwe can take it up right now. \nSHRI PAWAN KUMAR BANSAL: We have to pass both the Bills— the Lokpal \nBill and the Whistleblower's Protection Bill. \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: We will take it up after the Lokpal Bill. \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: What is the sense of the House? \n...(Interruption)... \n\n 116 \nSOME HON. MEMBERS: Lokpal, Lokpal ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: But, how many Bills will you pass tomorrow? You \nhave four Bills to pass! ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU (Karnataka): In the morning, first take up the \nLokpal Bill. ...(Interruptions)... इसम� आपित्त क्या है? ...(व्यवधान)... What is \nthe problem ? ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI PAWAN KUMAR BANSAL: Sir, the decision taken in the BAC was that \nthe House can sit up to eight, and if necessary, beyond eight; which \nmeans, today, given the time, it is quarter- to-five, we can clear the \nWhistleblower Bill today; and, tomorrow, for eight hours, we will have \nthe Lokpal Bill. ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: But the House is not in a position... \nSHRI PAWAN KUMAR BANSAL: Sir, tomorrow, we will have eight hours for \nthe Lokpal Bill. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: We are waiting since morning. We got only the \ncopies at 3 o'clock. Now, we have sent our amendments. But the \nSupplementary Agenda is not circulated. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI PAWAN KUMAR BANSAL: Sir, as an alternative, may I put it to the \nHouse, Sir, through you, and also to Shri Ahluwalia, that you may take up \nboth the Bills together tomorrow? \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: No, no. ...(Interruptions)... It was the \ndecision of the BAC to take it up separately. Sir, my submission is, \nyesterday, you have discussed both the Lokpal Bill and the Whistleblower \nBill. Now, show me a single instance where only one Member spoke about \nthe Whistleblower Bill, and that too was not responded to by the \nMinister. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI PAWAN KUMAR BANSAL: That is for the Members to speak. \n...(Interruptions)... It was for the Members to speak. \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: The point is not that. ...(Interruptions)... \nBoth the Bills should be discussed separately. ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: What we suggest is, let both the Bills be taken \nup together, but the Members will be given the opportunity to speak on \nboth the Bills. ...(Interruptions)... \n\n 117 \nSHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: No, Sir. The point is ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD (Bihar): Sir, the Whistleblower Bill is very \nimportant. It should be taken up separately. ...(Interruptions)... \nMR. DEPUTY CHAIRMAN: Yes, you will be given an 